The answer is: ...came down from the mountain. They were hungry, and they were thirsty. They came upon a village where a feast was being prepared. The people of the village took them in. They were fed and they were given something to drink. Then they were asked where they came from and where they were going. The two men said they were from Egulac and were on a vision quest. They were escorted to a nearby cave and given a burial place. Then they were gone. But before they left they warned the people of the village that a great flood was coming. The people of the village were either drowned or left the village. The two men had warned them that the flood would come from the west. The people of the village had ignored the warning.

This story is from the Ojibwe nation. It tells us that people from long ago, even if they were strangers, were welcomed and fed if they were hungry and thirsty. We do not know where Egulac is, but we can guess that it is far away, over the mountains, because the people of the village did not know where the two men came from. The story tells us that strangers should be fed and given shelter if they need it, even if we do not know where they come from. It also tells us that warnings of danger should be heeded, even if the warning comes from a stranger.


