The answer is: ...came down from the mountain. They were hungry, and they were thirsty. They came upon a village where a feast was being prepared. The people of the village took them in. They were fed and they were given something to drink. Then they were asked where they came from and where they were going. The two men said they were from Egulac and were on a vision quest. They were escorted to a nearby cave and given a burial place. Then they were gone. But before they left they warned the people of the village that a great flood was coming. The people of the village were either drowned or left the village. The two men had warned them of the coming disaster but the people did not listen.

This is a story from the oral tradition of the Ktunaxa people, told by Chief Joseph of the Ktunaxa Nation. It is a story of warning, of the importance of listening to others and to the signs in the natural world. It is a story that can be applied to many situations, including the current situation with climate change. The two men can be seen as representing the natural world, with the warning of the flood representing the need to take action to prevent environmental damage.


